*To the Reader*

The goal of this book is not to be as easy to read as possible.

Readability is an important objective in educational materials, but it is often pursued at the expense of precision. When concepts are oversimplified or definitions are replaced with everyday descriptions, understanding remains superficial. Moreover, when a textbook relies heavily on informal language and storytelling, students may find it difficult to distinguish between what is essential and what merely serves to illustrate a point.

This book is not intended to be read independently of classroom instruction. Although it is not meant to replace teaching, the text has been written as precisely and consistently as possible so that students can revisit the material and, when necessary, review and consolidate the fundamental concepts on their own.

In the early stages of learning physics, I believe that concepts are acquired through observation, measurement, and experimentation. During lessons, students investigate physical phenomena through hands-on activities. The concepts introduced in this book give those observations and methods precise meaning.

Once a solid foundation has been established for investigating physical phenomena and understanding the fundamental concepts, students can move on to the mathematical tools used to describe and explain them. This book does not shy away from a somewhat deeper mathematical treatment. Direct proportionality is introduced as a mathematical relationship between two physical quantities so that students can understand where the fundamental equations and mathematical models of physics originate. 

Many of the concepts introduced at the beginning of this book—such as model, a physical law, graph, and direct proportionality—reappear throughout the study of physics. Understanding these concepts helps students recognize that physics is not merely a collection of isolated formulas or explanations of individual phenomena, but a systematic mathematical way of investigating and understanding the natural world.

Warning! A teacher accustomed to relying on a traditional textbook may also be quite startled when reading this book. Where, for example, are the  exercises in which students determine how the ratio of the primary and secondary windings of a transformer affects the secondary voltage? My response is another question: is this really anything more than a rather tedious and technical calculation? Few of us will ever need to design battery chargers or AC/DC transformers. Wouldn't it be more relevant—and ultimately more educational—to examine, for instance, the energy losses of a mobile phone charger, as this book does?

The proportional relationship associated with transformers is certainly an interesting mathematical exercise and an opportunity to practise solving equations. But ultimately, I believe it is better to introduce direct proportionality as a rigorous mathematical relationship between two quantities, rather than presenting it merely as a formula-driven computational trick.

Geometrical optics, on the other hand, has been omitted entirely because it is technical and, frankly, rather dull—unless you happen to be an optician. If there is anything genuinely fascinating about optics, it is how we perceive colour, just as the fascinating aspect of sound is its perceptible properties: frequency, which we perceive as pitch, and the spectrum, which we perceive as timbre. Why, then, is the concept of the spectrum so conspicuously absent from virtually every elementary physics textbook I've seen?

I know the answer. In the 1960s there was simply no practical way to study the spectrum of sound or light in an ordinary classroom. Modern physics textbooks have largely become copies of one another, repeated edition after edition, and few authors have been willing to depart from the established canon. Today, however, analysing the spectrum of sound using nothing more than a laptop and a microphone is  trivial. Nowadays, even an entry-level spectrometer—or simply a handheld spectroscope—is within reach of many schools. This makes spectroscopy an accessible and engaging way for students to investigate the nature of light firsthand.

 The same tradition-defying philosophy has guided the entire book. The content has been modernized, and while some of the traditional mathematical treatment has been reduced, mathematics has been given a more prominent role wherever it provides a more precise way of understanding physical phenomena. This approach also supports differentiation for more advanced students whose mathematical skills allows them to engage with the material at a deeper level.

I hope that this book not only teaches the principles of physics, but also demonstrates how physicists—and, more broadly, scientists—think, what kinds of research methods they use, and how scientific knowledge is constructed and communicated.

The principles presented here extend far beyond physics. They form the foundation of modern science and shape the way knowledge is produced, evaluated, and applied in today's world. In doing so, they have profoundly influenced the modern worldview, even though we often take them for granted.

Finally, I would like to point out that this book was not the result of a sudden decision to write a physics textbook. It is the culmination of more than twenty years of teaching experience, during which the ideas presented in this book have been continuously developed, refined, and tested in the classroom.

The book reflects my own view of what is truly essential in physics at it's elementary level: not only in terms of content, but also in the way the subject should be taught. The aim is for students to develop a deeper understanding of physics rather than simply learning formulas, although, at times, some formulas inevitably have to be introduced as givens because students do not have the background needed to fully understand their origin. 

Readers are welcome to disagree with the approach taken in this book—that was the intention from the beginning. The goal was to preserve the traditional content of a lower secondary physics curriculum while rethinking how that content is presented, rather than producing another generic copy of existing textbooks.

_Author_
